   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 216
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0055",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "Since $\\psi$ is normalized and $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$ are orthogonal, we have $\\left|c_1\\right|^2 + \\left|c_2\\right|^2 = 1$. The probability of measuring $E_1$ is $\\left|c_1\\right|^2$ and $E_2$ is $\\left|c_2\\right|^2$. The average energy is given by:",
   "line": 217,
   "lines": [
    217,
    217
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 217
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 218
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 219
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "$$\\left<\\hat{H}\\right> = \\left<\\psi\\left|\\hat{H}\\right|\\psi\\right> = \\left|c_1\\right|^2\\left<\\phi_1\\left|\\hat{H}\\right|\\phi_1\\right> + c_1^*c_2\\left<\\phi_1\\left|\\hat{H}\\right|\\phi_2\\right> + c_2^*c_1\\left<\\phi_2\\left|\\hat{H}\\right|\\phi_1\\right>$$\n",
   "line": 220
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "$$ + \\left|c_2\\right|^2\\left<\\phi_2\\left|\\hat{H}\\right|\\phi_2\\right> = \\left|c_1\\right|^2E_1 + c_1^*c_2E_2\\underbrace{\\left<\\phi_1\\left|\\phi_2\\right.\\right>}_{= 0} + c_2^*c_1E_1\\underbrace{\\left<\\phi_2\\left|\\phi_1\\right.\\right>}_{= 0} + \\left|c_2\\right|^2E_2$$\n",
   "line": 221
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "$$= \\left|c_1\\right|^2E_1 + \\left|c_2\\right|^2E_2$$\n",
   "line": 222
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 223
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 224
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0056",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": " The standard deviation is given by : $\\sigma_{\\hat{H}} = \\sqrt{\\left<\\hat{H}^2\\right> - \\left<\\hat{H}\\right>^2}$. We have already calculated $\\left<\\hat{H}\\right>$ above and need to calculate $\\left<\\hat{H}^2\\right>$ (use the eigenvalue equation and orthogonality):",
   "line": 225,
   "lines": [
    225,
    225
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 225
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 226
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "$$\\left<\\hat{H}^2\\right> = \\left<\\psi\\left|\\hat{H}^2\\right|\\psi\\right> = \\left<\\psi\\left|\\hat{H}\\right|E_1c_1\\phi_1 + E_2c_2\\phi_2\\right> = \\left<c_1\\phi_1 + c_2\\phi_2\\left|E_1^2c_1\\phi_1 + E_2^2c_2\\phi_2\\right.\\right>$$\n",
   "line": 227
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "$$ = \\left|c_1\\right|^2E_1^2 + \\left|c_2\\right|^2E_2^2 \\Rightarrow \\sigma_{\\hat{H}} = \\sqrt{\\left|c_1\\right|^2E_1^2 + \\left|c_2\\right|^2E_2^2 - \\left(\\left|c_1\\right|^2E_1 + \\left|c_2\\right|^2E_2\\right)^2}$$\n",
   "line": 228
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 229
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": ":::\n",
   "line": 230
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 231
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 232
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 233
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "```{marimo-config}\n---\npyproject: |\n  requires-python = \">=3.10\"\n  dependencies = [\n      \"sympy\",\n  ]\n---\n```\n",
   "line": 234
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 243
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "```{marimo} python\n:hide-code: true\n\nimport marimo as mo\nimport sympy as sp\n```\n",
   "line": 244
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 250
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0057",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "The computer can carry the whole calculation symbolically. Watch $\\langle \\hat{p}^2 \\rangle$ come out of three live cells (each is editable):",
   "line": 251,
   "lines": [
    251,
    251
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 251
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 252
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "```{marimo} python\nx, L, n, hbar = sp.symbols(\"x L n hbar\", positive=True)\npsi_n = sp.sqrt(2 / L) * sp.sin(n * sp.pi * x / L)\npsi_n\n```\n",
   "line": 253
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 258
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "```{marimo} python\np2_psi = -hbar**2 * sp.diff(psi_n, x, 2)\np2_psi\n```\n",
   "line": 259
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 263
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "```{marimo} python\np2_avg = sp.simplify(sp.integrate(psi_n * p2_psi, (x, 0, L)))\np2_avg\n```\n",
   "line": 264
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 268
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0058",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "The result $\\langle p^2 \\rangle = n^2 \\pi^2 \\hbar^2 / L^2$ is exactly $2m E_n$: all of the particle in a box energy is kinetic.",
   "line": 269,
   "lines": [
    269,
    269
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 269
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 270
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "### ",
   "line": 271
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0059",
   "kind": "heading",
   "s": "Quantum states as linear superpositions of mutually exclusive states",
   "line": 271,
   "lines": [
    271
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 271
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 272
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "$$\\mid \\psi \\rangle = \\sum_n c_n \\mid \\phi_n \\rangle $$\n",
   "line": 273
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 274
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 275
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0060",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "In an experiment one always obtains one of the eigenvalues (see Postulates), corresponding to $\\phi_n$.",
   "line": 275,
   "lines": [
    275
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 275
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 276
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 277
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0061",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "In other words, the system described by a **superposition wavefunction \"collapses\" to one of the eigenfunctions** when the experiment is carried out. ",
   "line": 277,
   "lines": [
    277
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 277
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 278
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "  $$\\mid \\psi \\rangle \\rightarrow \\mid \\phi_n \\rangle$$\n",
   "line": 279
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 280
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 281
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0062",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "In experiments one only observes different eigenvalues, with probability given by the squared coefficients $\\mid c_n \\mid^2$.",
   "line": 281,
   "lines": [
    281
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 281
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 282
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 283
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0063",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "The idea of a quantum system randomly collapsing into distinct and mutually exclusive states troubled many of the physicists who were at the frontiers of the development of quantum mechanics. ",
   "line": 283,
   "lines": [
    283
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 283
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 284
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 285
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0064",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "**Orthogonality of eigenfunctions** means **mutually exclusive** states. For example, the system can only be in either state 1 or state 2, but not both.",
   "line": 285,
   "lines": [
    285
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 285
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 286
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "  $$\\langle \\phi_1 \\mid \\phi_2 \\rangle=0$$\n",
   "line": 287
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 288
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 289
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "### ",
   "line": 290
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0065",
   "kind": "heading",
   "s": "Copenhagen Interpretation",
   "line": 290,
   "lines": [
    290
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 290
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 291
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 292
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0066",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "The [Copenhagen interpretation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copenhagen_interpretation#cite_note-Siddiqui2017-1) is an expression of the meaning of [quantum mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_mechanics) that was largely devised from 1925 to 1927 by [Niels Bohr](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niels_Bohr) and [Werner Heisenberg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Werner_Heisenberg). It is one of the oldest of numerous proposed [interpretations of quantum mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interpretations_of_quantum_mechanics), and remains one of the most commonly taught.",
   "line": 292,
   "lines": [
    292
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 292
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 293
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 294
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0067",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "According to the Copenhagen interpretation, physical systems generally do not have definite properties prior to being measured, and quantum mechanics can only predict the probability distribution of a given measurement's possible results. ",
   "line": 294,
   "lines": [
    294
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 294
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "- ",
   "line": 295
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0068",
   "kind": "para",
   "s": "The act of measurement affects the system, causing the set of probabilities to reduce to only one of the possible values immediately after the measurement. This feature is known as [wave function collapse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_function_collapse).",
   "line": 295,
   "lines": [
    295
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 295
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 296
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 297
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "\n",
   "line": 298
  },
  {
   "t": "lit",
   "s": "### ",
   "line": 299
  },
  {
   "t": "tr",
   "id": "S0069",
   "kind": "heading",
   "s": "Quantum superposition of an atom",
